<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gommateshwara statue</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 983</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gommateshwara statue is a monolithic stone statue of the Jain figure Bahubali, about 17 metres high, on the hill of Vindhyagiri at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shravanabelagola">Shravanabelagola</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was commissioned around 983 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chavundaraya">Chavundaraya</a>, minister and general of the Western Ganga kings, and is one of the largest free-standing statues in the world. Every twelve years Jain pilgrims gather for the Mahamastakabhisheka, when the statue is anointed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gommateshwara-statue.jpg" alt="Gommateshwara statue" width="320" height="348" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kuldeep S · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gommateshwara,_Shravanabelagola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
